WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:12.530
In this video, you create a virtual private cloud with a subnet, then add a VPC NAT gateway so that virtual machines in the VPC can reach the internet.

00:00:12.530 --> 00:00:19.420
Under Overlay Networks, Virtual Private Cloud lists the VPCs. Select Create.

00:00:19.420 --> 00:00:22.470
Name the VPC demo-vpc.

00:00:22.470 --> 00:00:25.760
On the Static Routes tab, select Add.

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:31.690
Route all destinations, zero dot zero dot zero dot zero slash zero,

00:00:31.690 --> 00:00:41.000
to 172.31.10.254. The NAT gateway uses this address inside the VPC.

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:45.380
Select Create.

00:00:45.380 --> 00:00:50.670
In the demo-vpc group, select Create Subnet.

00:00:50.670 --> 00:00:53.880
Name the subnet demo-vpc-subnet.

00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:59.870
Enter the CIDR block 172.31.10.0/24.

00:00:59.870 --> 00:01:09.160
As the provider, select the overlay network demo-vpc-net. The VPC is already set to demo-vpc.

00:01:09.160 --> 00:01:13.860
Set the gateway IP to 172.31.10.1.

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:20.020
Enable DHCP, so that virtual machines get an address and a default route.

00:01:20.020 --> 00:01:26.210
Enable NAT Outgoing, as the documentation recommends for tenant subnets.

00:01:26.210 --> 00:01:28.660
Select Create.

00:01:28.660 --> 00:01:36.790
demo-vpc-subnet now belongs to demo-vpc. Without a gateway, it has no route out of the VPC.

00:01:36.790 --> 00:01:41.780
Next, open NAT and Internet, under Overlay Networks.

00:01:41.780 --> 00:01:44.410
Select Gateways,

00:01:44.410 --> 00:01:46.790
and then Create.

00:01:46.790 --> 00:01:49.730
Name the gateway demo-gw.

00:01:49.730 --> 00:01:54.700
Select the internal tenant network demo-vpc-net,

00:01:54.700 --> 00:01:58.360
the VPC demo-vpc,

00:01:58.360 --> 00:02:02.540
and the subnet demo-vpc-subnet.

00:02:02.540 --> 00:02:12.530
The LAN IP is the gateway's address in the subnet, 172.31.10.254, the next hop of the VPC route.

00:02:12.530 --> 00:02:15.450
Open the External Subnets tab.

00:02:15.450 --> 00:02:26.240
Select the external subnet demo-ext-subnet. In this lab, it stands in for an underlay VLAN with outbound NAT through the host.

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:29.980
Select Create.

00:02:29.980 --> 00:02:38.720
SUSE Virtualization starts the gateway as a pod with one leg in the VPC subnet and one in the external subnet.

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:44.560
Next, give the gateway an external IP. Select External IPs,

00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:46.950
and then Create.

00:02:46.950 --> 00:02:49.070
Name it demo-eip.

00:02:49.070 --> 00:02:53.520
Select the NAT gateway demo-gw,

00:02:53.520 --> 00:03:00.570
and the external subnet. Leave V4 IP empty to take the next free address.

00:03:00.570 --> 00:03:03.020
Select Create.

00:03:03.020 --> 00:03:07.780
Finally, add a source NAT rule. Select Source Rules,

00:03:07.780 --> 00:03:10.170
and then Create.

00:03:10.170 --> 00:03:12.430
Name it demo-snat.

00:03:12.430 --> 00:03:16.310
Select the external IP demo-eip.

00:03:16.310 --> 00:03:26.530
Enter the subnet CIDR, 172.31.10.0/24. Traffic from it leaves with the external IP as its source.

00:03:26.530 --> 00:03:30.570
Select Create.

00:03:30.570 --> 00:03:41.870
To test the path, a small virtual machine, demo-vpc-vm, was attached to the overlay network demo-vpc-net. Open Virtual Machines.

00:03:41.870 --> 00:03:48.750
Its address comes from demo-vpc-subnet, assigned by the subnet's DHCP service.

00:03:48.750 --> 00:03:51.340
Open its console,

00:03:51.340 --> 00:03:55.090
in WebVNC.

00:03:55.090 --> 00:03:57.260
Log in as sles,

00:03:57.260 --> 00:04:01.200
with the password that cloud-init set.

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:11.150
Ping a public address on the internet, 8.8.8.8.

00:04:11.150 --> 00:04:21.310
The replies arrive. Traffic leaves the VPC through demo-gw, with demo-eip as its source address.

00:04:21.310 --> 00:04:30.963
The virtual machine in demo-vpc reaches the internet through the NAT gateway. Next, you manage hosts out of band.
